because we attracted people that really appreciated

463
00:24:44,580 --> 00:24:46,220
those kind of more advanced features

464
00:24:46,220 --> 00:24:48,740
rather than just a deposit or just borrow.

465
00:24:48,740 --> 00:24:50,060
There's more you could do.

466
00:24:50,060 --> 00:24:53,460
The protocol is more efficient, I think, in many ways, more capital efficient.

467
00:24:53,460 --> 00:24:57,140
You can see that in the charts. There's higher overall utilisation.

468
00:24:57,140 --> 00:25:00,420
People tend to prefer borrowing on Euler than other platforms.

469
00:25:00,420 --> 00:25:02,140
In some scenarios, not all of them.

470
00:25:02,140 --> 00:25:03,940
Certainly, if you're borrowing from something like Aave,

471
00:25:03,940 --> 00:25:05,500
they have a lot more liquidity.

472
00:25:05,500 --> 00:25:07,540
Sometimes it's easier to do that because of that,

473
00:25:07,540 --> 00:25:12,180
but on the purely technical front, I think we make borrowing easy on Euler.

474
00:25:12,180 --> 00:25:15,900
We show people lots of information about their borrowing positions

475
00:25:15,900 --> 00:25:18,500
and statistics and things that aren't shown in other protocols,

476
00:25:18,500 --> 00:25:20,940
which help people manage risk a little better.

477
00:25:20,940 --> 00:25:22,700
We don't penalise people when they're liquidated.

478
00:25:22,700 --> 00:25:25,540
We try to minimise the costs from liquidations as much as possible.

479
00:25:25,540 --> 00:25:28,700
Whereas if you get liquidated on other protocols, it can be really devastating.

480
00:25:28,700 --> 00:25:30,820
You pretty much usually get wiped out, I would say.

481
00:25:30,820 --> 00:25:32,380
Whereas on Euler, that's not the case.

482
00:25:32,380 --> 00:25:34,260
You usually are able to keep almost...

483
00:25:34,260 --> 00:25:37,420
Most of the net asset value of the account is sort of retained

484
00:25:37,420 --> 00:25:40,940
after the liquidation rather than going to liquidation fees

485
00:25:40,940 --> 00:25:42,740
or liquidation penalties.

486
00:25:42,740 --> 00:25:45,620
So, yeah, I think borrowing overall has been a big thing

487
00:25:45,620 --> 00:25:48,740
that people tend to like more on other than other protocols.

488
00:25:48,740 --> 00:25:50,820
And when you build out product functionality

489
00:25:50,820 --> 00:25:53,380
or build out markets on the protocol,

490
00:25:53,380 --> 00:25:55,980
do you do it with a bit of a sandbox mentality

491
00:25:55,980 --> 00:25:57,820
of this is functionally something very good,

492
00:25:57,820 --> 00:26:00,180
or do you do it more with a view of,

493
00:26:00,180 --> 00:26:03,420
okay, people will do this because they'll want to go then do this on Pendle

494
00:26:03,420 --> 00:26:08,140
or EtherFi, or you're thinking about how those use cases are reported elsewhere?

495
00:26:08,140 --> 00:26:09,140
Good question.

496
00:26:09,140 --> 00:26:11,260
I said this is something that we learned from V1.

497
00:26:11,260 --> 00:26:14,460
Firstly, I'd say that just our engineering style

498
00:26:14,500 --> 00:26:16,700
is to always try to build things in this modular way.

499
00:26:16,700 --> 00:26:20,420
So we try to abstract things and then package them up into a lot of modules

500
00:26:20,420 --> 00:26:22,140
that you can then recombine.

501
00:26:22,140 --> 00:26:25,260
What's useful about that is that the things that come up

502
00:26:25,260 --> 00:26:28,140
that people end up asking you for, that you didn't necessarily anticipate,

503
00:26:28,140 --> 00:26:33,220
they can usually be done out of the box by then recombining the modules

504
00:26:33,220 --> 00:26:35,100
and allowing them to interact in different ways.

505
00:26:35,100 --> 00:26:38,180
So you can sort of use Euler as more of like a kit,

506
00:26:38,180 --> 00:26:40,500
a developer's kit for building products.

507
00:26:40,500 --> 00:26:42,060
So we don't start with the products as such.

508
00:26:42,060 --> 00:26:45,780
We start normally with the underlying engineering in mind.

509
00:26:45,780 --> 00:26:47,900
Some people say that's the wrong way to do things.

510
00:26:47,900 --> 00:26:51,420
Like many people advocate for building specific products for specific users

511
00:26:51,420 --> 00:26:54,620
and just tailoring to a particular use case.

512
00:26:54,620 --> 00:26:56,100
But I would say we do things the other way.

513
00:26:56,100 --> 00:26:59,860
We sort of build from the ground up and tend to try not to anticipate

514
00:26:59,860 --> 00:27:03,380
all the possible use cases, but assume that we can hopefully build

515
00:27:03,380 --> 00:27:06,900
any kind of product on top, any kind of credit-related product

516
00:27:06,900 --> 00:27:08,700
on top of the kit that we got Euler.

517
00:27:08,740 --> 00:27:13,100
So we hope to not have to keep releasing the V3, V4, V5 and so on.

518
00:27:13,100 --> 00:27:16,660
We want Euler to be as flexible enough to add new features,

519
00:27:16,660 --> 00:27:19,380
some of which I'm sure we'll talk about in the pod today.

520
00:27:19,380 --> 00:27:21,580
Yeah, so that's our general philosophy.

521
00:27:21,580 --> 00:27:25,460
What have you learned about user behavior over that time?

522
00:27:25,460 --> 00:27:26,460
Oh, it's changed massively.

523
00:27:26,460 --> 00:27:30,780
I mean, the use cases that people used to do in V1, mainly do in V1,

524
00:27:30,780 --> 00:27:33,260
are still there today, but there are many more use cases today.

525
00:27:33,260 --> 00:27:37,180
So in the early days of DeFi, people were mainly using lending protocols

526
00:27:37,220 --> 00:27:39,380
to borrow against volatile crypto assets.

527
00:27:39,380 --> 00:27:40,900
And they did that for a few different reasons.

528
00:27:40,900 --> 00:27:44,380
Sometimes they want to borrow against the ETH or Bitcoin

529
00:27:44,380 --> 00:27:45,660
because they want to go long.

530
00:27:45,660 --> 00:27:48,300
So they would like them to redeposit back into ETH or Bitcoin

531
00:27:48,300 --> 00:27:50,340
and amplify their exposure.

532
00:27:50,340 --> 00:27:52,940
Sometimes they wanted to use their assets in the real world.

533
00:27:52,940 --> 00:27:56,180
So they just borrow against it and then go buy a car or pay for something

534
00:27:56,180 --> 00:28:00,300
in the real world, or take advantage of another opportunity out there in DeFi.

535
00:28:00,300 --> 00:28:03,500
It was mostly loans against volatile assets in the early days.

536
00:28:03,500 --> 00:28:06,500
And I think in that change with state ETH, when state ETH emerged,

537
00:28:06,500 --> 00:28:07,820
we had yield-bearing forms of ETH.

538
00:28:07,820 --> 00:28:10,340
And that was like the first time that we really started to see growth

539
00:28:10,340 --> 00:28:13,220
of a different type of trade, which was more of like a carriage trade.

540
00:28:13,220 --> 00:28:16,340
We deposit something that's high yield, then borrow something that's low yielding.

541
00:28:16,340 --> 00:28:19,700
And then you can scale up or down your exposure to the yield,

542
00:28:19,700 --> 00:28:23,420
basically by redepositing back into the yield-bearing asset.

543
00:28:23,420 --> 00:28:27,100
And that's just completely exploded as the main use case,

544
00:28:27,100 --> 00:28:30,980
I'd say, for lending borrowing protocols in the past 12 months,

545
00:28:30,980 --> 00:28:34,940
which is why protocols like Euler and also Morpho have done ever so well.

546
00:28:34,980 --> 00:28:37,500
During that time, it's just that these kind of carriage trades

547
00:28:37,500 --> 00:28:39,700
are now the main use case for lending protocols, I would say.

548
00:28:39,700 --> 00:28:44,060
People are arbitraging interest rates and capturing yield from stables,

549
00:28:44,060 --> 00:28:46,620
rather than just borrowing against volatile assets.

550
00:28:46,620 --> 00:28:49,500
Which I look at as positive, to go back to the root of our discussion.

551
00:28:49,500 --> 00:28:52,340
It's market efficiency rate discovery.

552
00:28:52,340 --> 00:28:53,540
Yeah, I mean, it's interesting.

553
00:28:53,540 --> 00:28:57,540
I think there's this tendency a lot in foul finance,

554
00:28:57,540 --> 00:29:00,140
where you have assets that are volatile.

555
00:29:00,140 --> 00:29:04,660
And what people want to do is they try to compress that volatility

556
00:29:04,660 --> 00:29:06,100
as best they can.

557
00:29:06,100 --> 00:29:07,860
Now, you can always compress volatility,

558
00:29:07,860 --> 00:29:11,340
but you can't, in my opinion, fully remove it from the system.

559
00:29:11,340 --> 00:29:14,700
So, you know, if you've got something that's volatile over 12 months,

560
00:29:14,700 --> 00:29:16,820
you can make it less volatile over 12 months,

561
00:29:16,820 --> 00:29:19,700
but there might be one really big spike in that time.

562
00:29:19,700 --> 00:29:22,820
And I think that's kind of what's happening with the change in crypto today.

563
00:29:22,820 --> 00:29:24,060
Things like Athena and so on,

564
00:29:24,060 --> 00:29:27,980
we're still benefiting from the yields on some of these volatile assets and things,

565
00:29:27,980 --> 00:29:31,900
but we're finding ways to effectively compress the volatility

566
00:29:31,900 --> 00:29:34,580
into products that are more predictable, I would say.

567
00:29:34,620 --> 00:29:36,060
At least on short timeframes.

568
00:29:36,060 --> 00:29:38,620
And then people focus on trading the rates with those,

569
00:29:38,620 --> 00:29:41,300
rather than focusing on directional trading,

570
00:29:41,300 --> 00:29:43,020
where things are going to go up or down.

571
00:29:43,020 --> 00:29:45,220
They try and turn that into a yield-to-bend product instead.

572
00:29:45,220 --> 00:29:48,180
So I think that's like a common trend and overall, probably a good thing.

573
00:29:48,180 --> 00:29:52,660
But one downside to it maybe is that it allows for more leverage

574
00:29:52,660 --> 00:29:55,780
to build in the system and like less predictability in the long term.

575
00:29:56,140 --> 00:29:58,260
If you wanted to go leverage long on Bitcoin,

576
00:29:58,260 --> 00:30:01,020
there's probably a ceiling on the amount of leverage you could take.

577
00:30:01,140 --> 00:30:02,620
Let's say it's 5x or something, right?

578
00:30:02,620 --> 00:30:05,140
There's like a sane amount of leverage that you can potentially take

579
00:30:05,140 --> 00:30:06,580
if you earn some Bitcoin.

580
00:30:06,580 --> 00:30:07,980
I want it to go long.

581
00:30:07,980 --> 00:30:10,380
But if you have a stablecoin that's yield-bearing,

582
00:30:10,380 --> 00:30:14,260
maybe you get 20, 30, 40x leverage out of that stablecoin.

583
00:30:15,260 --> 00:30:18,260
And yeah, over a short time frame, that might be perfectly fine.

584
00:30:18,260 --> 00:30:22,540
When it does eventually unwind, it can do so maybe even more validly

585
00:30:22,540 --> 00:30:25,780
than the people who are trading on leverage on Bitcoin.

586
00:30:25,780 --> 00:30:28,940
So yeah, I think there's trade-offs with the way the industry is moving.

587
00:30:29,060 --> 00:30:32,660
But yeah, certainly stablecoins and yield-bearing assets

588
00:30:33,020 --> 00:30:35,180
work quite well with lending protocols, I'd say.

589
00:30:35,180 --> 00:30:37,860
And the two have very strong quarter market fit with one another.

590
00:30:38,340 --> 00:30:41,060
I want to just highlight that point because I think for people

591
00:30:41,060 --> 00:30:43,860
who aren't active in DeFi or crypto,

592
00:30:44,260 --> 00:30:47,300
one of the most underappreciated aspects of the market

593
00:30:47,300 --> 00:30:50,380
is how speculative people are on stablecoins

594
00:30:50,940 --> 00:30:52,860
through looping to the point you make.

595
00:30:52,860 --> 00:30:56,620
I think people think of speculation as being on meme coins and altcoins,

596
00:30:56,620 --> 00:30:59,060
but I fully agree on the point that you're making

597
00:30:59,060 --> 00:31:01,100
that people are speculating on stablecoins.

598
00:31:01,540 --> 00:31:02,980
Yeah, it's a huge trade.

599
00:31:02,980 --> 00:31:05,460
But it's the same in traditional finances, too, right?

600
00:31:05,460 --> 00:31:08,140
It's nothing unique to our industry.

601
00:31:08,380 --> 00:31:12,140
Yeah, trading rates, basically, and leveraging stablecoin yield,

602
00:31:12,140 --> 00:31:14,660
I think is probably the biggest trade in the market today.

603
00:31:14,780 --> 00:31:15,980
Maybe it's already the biggest.

604
00:31:15,980 --> 00:31:19,940
Certainly, if it's not today, I expect it will be in the nearest future.

605
00:31:20,140 --> 00:31:23,180
To touch on another piece of this related market structure,

606
00:31:23,700 --> 00:31:24,900
you've probably seen the same.

607
00:31:24,900 --> 00:31:29,820
We've seen a lot of these new CDP or collateralized

608
00:31:29,820 --> 00:31:34,140
debt position protocols coming about, which to me makes it clear

609
00:31:34,140 --> 00:31:37,580
that people have a desire to hold exposure to their altcoins,

610
00:31:37,940 --> 00:31:42,300
but to essentially park them somewhere and instead do all their financial things

611
00:31:42,300 --> 00:31:46,300
with a stablecoin, which gets to your point of they want to take

612
00:31:46,300 --> 00:31:50,540
whatever stablecoin they've now received and maybe go to rate arbitrage

613
00:31:50,540 --> 00:31:52,220
on it with a different stablecoin.

614
00:31:52,220 --> 00:31:54,820
My question seeing this is, do you think eventually

615
00:31:54,820 --> 00:31:59,620
we get to a point where the altcoins almost become more idle

616
00:31:59,620 --> 00:32:03,700
and most of actual DeFi finance happens using stablecoins?

617
00:32:03,820 --> 00:32:06,700
And I say this, seeing you go on Euler or Morpho

618
00:32:07,140 --> 00:32:10,060
or on these platforms now, the top assets are stablecoins.

619
00:32:10,060 --> 00:32:11,420
They didn't used to be that way.

620
00:32:11,420 --> 00:32:15,940
So I'm curious if you think that is the vehicle that DeFi is happening with,

621
00:32:16,140 --> 00:32:18,380
at least from a transaction perspective, long term.

622
00:32:19,220 --> 00:32:20,820
Yeah, I think so. I think it is.

623
00:32:20,820 --> 00:32:24,660
It's just much easier to move these stablecoins around and also to handle them

624
00:32:24,660 --> 00:32:25,500
and to price them.

625
00:32:25,500 --> 00:32:27,300
And so, like I say, there's no free lunch.

626
00:32:27,300 --> 00:32:30,860
I do think that over a longer time horizon, we will see some of them

627
00:32:31,300 --> 00:32:33,380
DPEG quite violently or wind.

628
00:32:33,380 --> 00:32:35,380
So there are definitely challenges with these things.

629
00:32:35,380 --> 00:32:37,460
There's no free lunch in finance ever.

630
00:32:37,460 --> 00:32:40,660
But they simplify the day to day experience rather than holding

631
00:32:40,820 --> 00:32:42,220
strictly volatile assets.

632
00:32:42,220 --> 00:32:44,860
And I think that's why a lot of traders prefer these things

633
00:32:45,460 --> 00:32:47,420
and why we're seeing so many step-by-step growth

634
00:32:47,420 --> 00:32:49,540
in that particular sector at the moment.

635
00:32:49,540 --> 00:32:54,140
Thinking over the longer term, it feels like non-USD stablecoins

636
00:32:54,140 --> 00:32:57,300
or FX markets could be something that could be interesting

637
00:32:57,300 --> 00:32:58,620
for a platform like Euler.

638
00:32:58,620 --> 00:33:01,500
Do you see that as being a potential DeFi reality?

639
00:33:02,300 --> 00:33:05,300
I do over some time horizon, but it's not today.

640
00:33:05,660 --> 00:33:06,340
Why is that?

641
00:33:06,340 --> 00:33:09,100
I mean, I've talked to people a lot about this particular topic

642
00:33:09,100 --> 00:33:12,420
and whether or not people will start creating FX like markets on Euler

643
00:33:13,340 --> 00:33:14,300
and now other swap.

644
00:33:14,300 --> 00:33:15,820
But yeah, there's been some challenge.

645
00:33:15,820 --> 00:33:18,100
I mean, like the dollar is just so powerful.

646
00:33:18,100 --> 00:33:20,780
It's the currency that everybody likes to use.

647
00:33:20,980 --> 00:33:22,700
We use for international trade.

648
00:33:22,700 --> 00:33:25,980
No one wants to hold euros, it seems, on chain at the moment.

649
00:33:25,980 --> 00:33:28,700
I think Circle have made a really big push with C

650
00:33:28,700 --> 00:33:30,820
and it hasn't really taken off today.

651
00:33:30,820 --> 00:33:32,180
I don't think it'll be like that forever.

652
00:33:32,180 --> 00:33:34,300
I think at some point they will start to move

653
00:33:34,300 --> 00:33:36,780
and we will see more FX like trading.

654
00:33:37,460 --> 00:33:42,460
I mean, there's more and more assets are coming on chain all the time.

655
00:33:42,460 --> 00:33:45,940
We're seeing stocks, equities and tokenized funds and everything.

656
00:33:45,940 --> 00:33:48,100
So it'd be strange to me if FX doesn't grow.

657
00:33:48,620 --> 00:33:52,460
But there are some disadvantages with on chain trading, off chain FX.

658
00:33:52,940 --> 00:33:55,220
The amount of leverage that people can take is absolutely huge

659
00:33:55,220 --> 00:33:57,380
and the order books and everything very, very efficient

660
00:33:57,380 --> 00:34:00,860
and things are a little less efficient on chain, I would say, at least today.

661
00:34:00,860 --> 00:34:03,700
That makes it a little harder perhaps to trade these things

662
00:34:03,700 --> 00:34:05,620
as profitably as he can do with off chain.

663
00:34:05,620 --> 00:34:08,100
So maybe that's why they're yet to take off.

664
00:34:08,820 --> 00:34:10,020
But some things just take time.

665
00:34:10,020 --> 00:34:11,220
We look at Pendle, right?

666
00:34:11,220 --> 00:34:14,140
Another protocol like ourselves that has been around for a while

667
00:34:14,140 --> 00:34:16,580
and it didn't really do an awful lot until one day it did.

668
00:34:16,580 --> 00:34:19,460
And it's one of the most important protocols in DeFi today.

669
00:34:19,940 --> 00:34:24,740
But for at least a year, maybe longer, fixed rate protocols were everyone would say.

670
00:34:24,740 --> 00:34:26,420
There's just no appetite for it in crypto.

671
00:34:26,420 --> 00:34:29,780
And I think you see that with the product lines as well, you know, options

672
00:34:30,260 --> 00:34:33,500
not really come to crypto yet, not really come to DeFi in a major way.

673
00:34:34,100 --> 00:34:37,140
But yeah, some things just take time and you reach a critical mass

674
00:34:37,140 --> 00:34:39,460
and then they just really take off and bloom after that.

675
00:34:39,460 --> 00:34:43,020
So, yeah, I wouldn't rule it out for FX, but it's not there today.

676
00:34:43,380 --> 00:34:46,100
That's a good segue talking about some of these functionalities

677
00:34:46,100 --> 00:34:49,060
that maybe haven't played out and some of those areas of the market

678
00:34:49,060 --> 00:34:49,980
that you can explore.

679
00:34:49,980 --> 00:34:54,140
What's your vision for Euler as a platform over the longer term?

680
00:34:55,020 --> 00:35:00,460
I think it should be a liquidity hub, a kind of a DeFi super app effectively,

681
00:35:00,460 --> 00:35:05,060
where it holds significant liquidity for people and provides a lot of functionality

682
00:35:05,060 --> 00:35:09,380
for doing different types of trades or different types of financial operations

683
00:35:09,380 --> 00:35:10,820
on top of that liquidity.

684
00:35:10,820 --> 00:35:13,300
So the underlying technology is very flexible.

685
00:35:13,300 --> 00:35:16,580
It allows you to build all sorts of different types of credit products on top,

686
00:35:16,940 --> 00:35:21,060
whether that's variable or fixed rate is something that we got coming in the pipeline.

687
00:35:21,780 --> 00:35:24,180
And I think fixed rates are going to come back in a very big way

688
00:35:24,180 --> 00:35:25,980
in DeFi in the fairly near future.

689
00:35:25,980 --> 00:35:28,780
I think all the major lending protocols are taking a look at this

690
00:35:28,780 --> 00:35:31,220
and rethinking how it should be done.

691
00:35:31,220 --> 00:35:34,300
So it will be interesting to see how that changes in the near future.

692
00:35:34,300 --> 00:35:37,620
But yeah, certainly you can build a very good fixed rate protocol to Boiler.

693
00:35:37,620 --> 00:35:39,220
I think that will be coming.

694
00:35:39,220 --> 00:35:42,260
On the swap is something we added fairly recently and it's still in beta,

695
00:35:42,260 --> 00:35:45,940
but it's been looking extremely promising as a new technology on top of Euler.

696
00:35:46,340 --> 00:35:48,900
Can you talk about how Euler swap works?

697
00:35:48,900 --> 00:35:50,260
I'm very excited about it.

698
00:35:50,260 --> 00:35:54,260
I mean, as a backdrop to say about some of the pain points of existing decentralized

699
00:35:54,260 --> 00:35:59,060
exchanges, one of which is that when you put liquidity in a DEX and it's not traded on,

700
00:35:59,060 --> 00:36:02,900
it's not utilized within the given block, it's just tidal liquidity.

701
00:36:02,900 --> 00:36:05,220
And that's just wildly inefficient to do that.

702
00:36:05,220 --> 00:36:08,580
Even on blocks where you have trades, most of the liquidity isn't utilized,

703
00:36:08,580 --> 00:36:10,020
just a small percentage of it is.

704
00:36:10,660 --> 00:36:12,260
That's just so inefficient to do that.

705
00:36:12,260 --> 00:36:15,540
The rest of that liquidity should be earning yield in some form or another.

706
00:36:15,540 --> 00:36:16,980
And that's how it works in TradFi.

707
00:36:16,980 --> 00:36:19,540
If you're trading on some off-chain order book,

708
00:36:19,540 --> 00:36:23,620
the liquidity isn't literally sat there in units being moved around in the order book.

709
00:36:23,620 --> 00:36:25,940
It's actually sat in the background somewhere in some account,

710
00:36:26,580 --> 00:36:27,540
earning yield for people.

711
00:36:27,540 --> 00:36:31,220
And then they set a lot of the order books at the end of the day or whatever.

712
00:36:31,220 --> 00:36:35,860
And Euler swap solves that problem, effectively pulls the liquidity inside

713
00:36:35,860 --> 00:36:40,100
lending or credit markets by default, optional for ALP.

714
00:36:40,100 --> 00:36:42,340
But by default, that's the assumed position.

715
00:36:42,340 --> 00:36:47,860
And then liquidity is then used just in time to supply liquidity to the market for swaps.

716
00:36:47,860 --> 00:36:53,300
And so when you LP on Euler swap, you earn yield by default from lending and borrowing first.

717
00:36:53,300 --> 00:36:56,740
And then if you want, you can also then switch on the option to earn swap fees

718
00:36:56,740 --> 00:36:59,060
and then extra sources of income from the swapping.

719
00:36:59,060 --> 00:37:01,700
So it's just much more capital efficient from that standpoint.

720
00:37:01,700 --> 00:37:06,180
But another major pain point of DexMarkets is borrowing against your liquidity.

721
00:37:06,900 --> 00:37:09,140
People want to do this for all sorts of reasons,

722
00:37:09,140 --> 00:37:12,100
but it's just not really been a solved problem.

723
00:37:12,100 --> 00:37:14,980
In my entire time in DeFi, people have always wanted to

724
00:37:14,980 --> 00:37:17,620
see if they could borrow against their Uniswap LP tokens.

725
00:37:18,340 --> 00:37:22,020
I remember people trying to do it in V2 and it didn't work there because it was very hard

726
00:37:22,020 --> 00:37:25,940
to price the tokens and there were all sorts of exploits associated with the Uniswap V3.

727
00:37:25,940 --> 00:37:30,660
It's arguably even harder because now the tokens were NFTs and they were even harder to price

728
00:37:30,660 --> 00:37:35,300
or to be able to use this collateral in a fungible type of collateral lending protocol.

729
00:37:35,300 --> 00:37:40,420
So Euler swap to Dex where the liquidity by default sits inside credit vaults.

730
00:37:40,740 --> 00:37:43,700
If those credit vaults are accepted as collateral in unsigned other vaults,

731
00:37:44,340 --> 00:37:47,620
then that gives them native borrowing functionality.

732
00:37:47,620 --> 00:37:52,820
So when you LP and Euler swap, not only do you earn interest on the assets from lending if you

733
00:37:52,820 --> 00:37:56,100
want, but you can also natively borrow against your LP positions.

734
00:37:56,100 --> 00:38:01,700
And this doesn't require any fancy logic in terms of moving the LP tokens around into new markets.

735
00:38:01,700 --> 00:38:04,820
I think it's just native functionality you get out of the box.

736
00:38:04,820 --> 00:38:08,660
So ultimately what happens is, let's say you want to LP ETH and USDC,

737
00:38:09,300 --> 00:38:12,420
you put ETH into an ETH credit vault and then some interest there.

738
00:38:12,420 --> 00:38:15,300
You put USDC into another credit vault and interest there.

739
00:38:15,300 --> 00:38:19,460
And then you as the account holder would install like an AMM curve on top of your account.

740
00:38:20,660 --> 00:38:24,900
It's kind of a bonding curve that defines the rules that swappers have to obey.

741
00:38:24,900 --> 00:38:28,500
And the swappers, what they're doing when they come in and swap is they're effectively just

742
00:38:28,500 --> 00:38:32,020
rebalancing the amount of ETH and USDC in your credit accounts now.

743
00:38:32,020 --> 00:38:36,660
Someone sells ETH, you get more ETH and you take some USDC out and vice versa.

744
00:38:37,300 --> 00:38:39,860
And if you want to borrow now against that position,

745
00:38:39,860 --> 00:38:43,540
you can because ETH and USDC are collaterals in Euler and lots of our markets.

746
00:38:43,540 --> 00:38:46,180
And so you can just go away and borrow against that position.

747
00:38:46,740 --> 00:38:48,580
Now, what might you do by borrowing?

748
00:38:48,580 --> 00:38:51,300
You could maybe borrow and supply even more liquidity.

749
00:38:51,300 --> 00:38:56,180
So you can sort of leverage the LP token and multiply the yields that you're earning.

750
00:38:56,180 --> 00:38:57,860
You might just borrow against it as a hedge.

751
00:38:57,860 --> 00:39:00,420
You might want to hedge out the ETH side of the position.

752
00:39:00,420 --> 00:39:03,220
And I think you don't want to be as exposed to that side of things.

753
00:39:03,860 --> 00:39:08,580
You might want to borrow just to access funds and liquidity to go use elsewhere in the EFI

754
00:39:08,580 --> 00:39:09,860
or some of the strategy.

755
00:39:09,860 --> 00:39:12,660
But yeah, lots of reasons to borrow against things.

756
00:39:12,660 --> 00:39:15,060
And it all comes out of the box with EulerSwap for free.

757
00:39:15,700 --> 00:39:19,700
Yeah, it's a very, very powerful rebalancing primitive people's accounts on top of Euler.

758
00:39:19,700 --> 00:39:23,940
And yeah, I think it's going to completely change the way that the DEXs work.

759
00:39:24,580 --> 00:39:27,940
We launched some beta testing faults using the Dow's liquidity.

760
00:39:27,940 --> 00:39:30,820
And in under two weeks, I think we've done over a billion dollars in

761
00:39:31,300 --> 00:39:33,860
trading volume on the USDC USDT pair.

762
00:39:34,500 --> 00:39:39,060
One of the reasons for that was because when people swap, the swap sometimes can be used

763
00:39:39,060 --> 00:39:41,620
as collateral to borrow the output amount.

764
00:39:41,620 --> 00:39:46,340
So an LP position of a million dollars can be used to simulate the same effective liquidity

765
00:39:46,340 --> 00:39:48,980
as a $50 million LP position in Uniswap.

766
00:39:49,620 --> 00:39:52,020
And then you leverage swap fees and everything on top.

767
00:39:52,020 --> 00:39:56,820
But you just provide overall much deeper liquidity by using the borrowing functionality of the LPD

768
00:39:56,820 --> 00:39:57,540
tokens.

769
00:39:57,620 --> 00:40:01,220
So yeah, it's a very, very, very powerful new primitive, but I'm very optimistic about it.

770
00:40:02,100 --> 00:40:06,180
And I see one immediate use case given everything we talked about.

771
00:40:06,180 --> 00:40:09,060
It'll probably be a great stable swap, I imagine, right?

772
00:40:09,060 --> 00:40:11,220
Because of all the stablecoin liquidity that you have.

773
00:40:11,780 --> 00:40:16,340
Yeah, hugely beneficial for new projects that are starting stablecoins as well.

774
00:40:16,340 --> 00:40:19,540
I mean, the current system for most projects starting a stablecoin today

775
00:40:19,540 --> 00:40:23,540
is the very first thing they need to do is somehow get DEX liquidity.

776
00:40:24,260 --> 00:40:25,700
How do they get that DEX liquidity?

777
00:40:25,700 --> 00:40:27,140
Well, they often have to pay for it.

778
00:40:27,140 --> 00:40:31,220
People often ask me, why is LPing on oil or swap immune to permanent loss and all

779
00:40:31,220 --> 00:40:32,740
these toxic flow and all this of it?

780
00:40:32,740 --> 00:40:35,140
And the answer is no, that's not where the innovation is.

781
00:40:35,140 --> 00:40:39,780
People aren't LPing all the time because they are profitable LPing just on the swap fees.

782
00:40:40,500 --> 00:40:45,140
People are LPing stablecoins because they need DEX liquidity because without the DEX liquidity,

783
00:40:45,140 --> 00:40:49,220
it's hard for them then to go on and build credit markets for their stablecoins.

784
00:40:49,220 --> 00:40:52,420
And if they don't have a credit market, then the stablecoin isn't that useful, really.

785
00:40:52,420 --> 00:40:54,740
So DEX liquidity comes first and foremost.

786
00:40:54,740 --> 00:41:00,180
What's unique about oil swap, I think, is you can build a credit market in parallel

787
00:41:00,180 --> 00:41:04,980
to a DEX market and you can actually do the whole thing in one go in a much more

788
00:41:04,980 --> 00:41:07,220
capital efficient way than people have done this in the past.

789
00:41:07,220 --> 00:41:11,540
You don't have to spend weeks building liquidity on a DEX and pay millions of

790
00:41:11,540 --> 00:41:15,780
dollars to incentivize that liquidity either from private LPs or from public LPs.

791
00:41:16,580 --> 00:41:20,260
You can effectively deposit the assets, collateralize them, go and borrow from a

792
00:41:20,340 --> 00:41:24,900
USDC market, for instance, the deposit so that you get a leveraged LP token.

793
00:41:24,900 --> 00:41:26,820
You'd now grow the DEX liquidity.

794
00:41:26,820 --> 00:41:30,900
Once you have DEX liquidity, you can now connect that to all the vaults and other

795
00:41:30,900 --> 00:41:33,460
things in the system where you can start to grow your credit market.

796
00:41:33,460 --> 00:41:37,860
It can all be done in a matter of days or a few weeks rather than taking months and months

797
00:41:37,860 --> 00:41:40,020
and costing millions of dollars to bootstrap things.

798
00:41:40,020 --> 00:41:42,260
It's just much, much cheaper and faster.

799
00:41:42,260 --> 00:41:46,020
Yeah, I think it's brilliant and inevitably we'll see a lot more

800
00:41:46,020 --> 00:41:47,620
stablecoins having to bootstrap.

801
00:41:47,620 --> 00:41:49,780
And so it's, to your point, really attractive for them.

802
00:41:50,420 --> 00:41:53,940
I want to touch on a couple more questions, maybe pivoting a little bit.

803
00:41:53,940 --> 00:41:57,860
Do you still have a desire to build out these primitives?

804
00:41:57,860 --> 00:42:00,740
Is this what you want to do sort of for your infinite game?

805
00:42:00,740 --> 00:42:02,660
Or do you ever want to go back to academia?

806
00:42:02,660 --> 00:42:05,460
Or how do you assess where you'd like to spend your time?

807
00:42:06,580 --> 00:42:10,020
I really enjoy the engineering side of the industry we work in.

808
00:42:10,020 --> 00:42:11,380
Yeah, I really, really enjoy it.

809
00:42:11,380 --> 00:42:15,300
I mean, most of all, the swap was my engineering ideas and things.

810
00:42:15,300 --> 00:42:18,980
So using stuff from my background, doing a lot of maths and things.

811
00:42:18,980 --> 00:42:20,900
So I really enjoy that side of things.

812
00:42:20,900 --> 00:42:24,980
Yeah, running in companies is definitely also challenging and quite stressful,

813
00:42:24,980 --> 00:42:25,300
I would say.

814
00:42:25,300 --> 00:42:27,860
It's certainly a very different experience from being an academic

815
00:42:27,860 --> 00:42:29,620
where you just work very much.

816
00:42:29,620 --> 00:42:32,660
Well, you work as part of the team, but you're kind of in charge of your own things

817
00:42:32,660 --> 00:42:36,180
and you don't necessarily have loads of people to answer to and people's livelihoods

818
00:42:36,180 --> 00:42:37,620
and the things dependent on you and so on.

819
00:42:37,620 --> 00:42:38,660
So yeah, it's quite different.

820
00:42:38,660 --> 00:42:40,820
I wouldn't go back to academia personally.

821
00:42:40,820 --> 00:42:43,780
A division about what academia being an academic will be like

822
00:42:43,780 --> 00:42:45,620
that didn't really play out when I was there.

823
00:42:45,620 --> 00:42:49,700
I enjoy some aspects of it doing the core scientific research I really enjoyed,

824
00:42:49,700 --> 00:42:51,780
much like I enjoyed in engineering stuff at OILA.

825
00:42:51,780 --> 00:42:56,100
But the day-to-day stuff, the amount of bureaucracy involved in being an academic is enormous.

826
00:42:56,100 --> 00:42:58,900
It's more about writing research grants and writing papers

827
00:42:58,900 --> 00:43:01,300
than it is about doing core research, I would say, these days.

828
00:43:01,940 --> 00:43:06,980
I think that whole industry probably has some evolving to do

829
00:43:06,980 --> 00:43:08,900
in order to keep up with the way the world's working.

830
00:43:08,900 --> 00:43:10,900
I think probably AI is changing a lot of things,

831
00:43:10,900 --> 00:43:13,220
but we'll completely shake up academia as well.

832
00:43:13,220 --> 00:43:17,060
I wouldn't go back, but I still sort of romanticize some elements of it, I suppose.

833
00:43:17,060 --> 00:43:19,140
Just being able to sit down and just chew through you,

834
00:43:19,140 --> 00:43:22,660
like challenging, problem-solving stuff day in, day out.

835
00:43:22,660 --> 00:43:23,460
I think it's really fun.

836
00:43:24,020 --> 00:43:26,820
The point you make on having to lead a protocol,

837
00:43:26,820 --> 00:43:30,420
in some ways you personally become the victim of your own success, right?

838
00:43:30,420 --> 00:43:32,980
Because the larger you get, the more attention you have,

839
00:43:32,980 --> 00:43:35,380
the more commitments you have, the more inbound you get.

840
00:43:36,020 --> 00:43:39,140
How do you balance the rest of your life with that reality

841
00:43:39,140 --> 00:43:42,100
and your family and alongside your commitments to the protocol?

842
00:43:42,820 --> 00:43:43,700
It's a good one, yeah.

843
00:43:44,820 --> 00:43:46,420
I'm not going to lie, it's quite challenging,

844
00:43:46,420 --> 00:43:48,900
but families are a natural balancer, I think.

845
00:43:48,900 --> 00:43:51,140
Before you have a family, you always think,

